#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369434 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fe866fa-6339-5f10-8ce3-3d5938c3785e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369436} {:answer/id #uuid "0aafa5fa-6806-5c1a-bd6e-a8f0ec680d0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369437} {:answer/id #uuid "b169f03c-00d2-5df3-887b-9ea86b4a1f34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369438} {:answer/id #uuid "2e03cd88-a4dd-5d49-b397-096aeb5b4219" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369439} {:answer/id #uuid "8e91bdb9-02ae-5c29-aa67-a875b2bc2112" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369440}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e03cd88-a4dd-5d49-b397-096aeb5b4219" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369439} :answer-field/id #uuid "d2ee2d3e-bbe0-5737-8885-1738f355a09b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369435}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "32a18b85-d978-5b18-8fff-914bdccf82dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-126853" :question/problem "![](images/39/393bed476f2e8d84fc7e5dc721d77749f7f259834e03a94a48a08533ae9befa6.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}2y\\,dx+4x\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=2y$ and $Q(x,y)=4x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}2y\\,dx+4x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4-2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (6\\cdot 4-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (24-4) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 20 \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369441 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "992dce16-160e-53f6-8630-c0af64cae1cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369443} {:answer/id #uuid "2bfd9130-7a02-5b45-a0d5-85f79505dd79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369444} {:answer/id #uuid "2ce07f1c-821c-51aa-8553-62af6bab118d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369445} {:answer/id #uuid "905e612c-8067-5089-bf8b-dc0ca7481414" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369446} {:answer/id #uuid "bae5a3fa-eb19-574d-b4eb-7f975d579bd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369447}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2bfd9130-7a02-5b45-a0d5-85f79505dd79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369444} :answer-field/id #uuid "37fee2c5-b026-5dca-a84d-1a957d12aae4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369442}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7aa45530-2cd6-5788-83f4-81c4c36b6f4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-126856" :question/problem "![](images/6b/6b52e4f2f37d6ba6971fbc8f6e1d610ab7e0c9d11341ea3b6a6772bff8f40e37.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}3y\\,dx+2x\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=3y$ and $Q(x,y)=2x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}3y\\,dx+2x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2-3)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-1\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-\\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =-(\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2}-\\pi \\cdot {1}^{2}) \\\\  & =-(9\\pi -\\pi ) \\\\  & =-8\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369448 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bd2f859-8cbd-5fb2-a799-4047912f2b9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369450} {:answer/id #uuid "c80ede24-c0d9-5ecf-be0b-3875c8087b10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369451} {:answer/id #uuid "b67159a4-a2c9-5c6e-a51b-b9e8fec0e05d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369452} {:answer/id #uuid "6d280bef-8091-5d89-be52-181cdb57aaa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369453} {:answer/id #uuid "aa5c20b2-9d53-5d85-af6f-b027ed46706b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369454}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c80ede24-c0d9-5ecf-be0b-3875c8087b10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369451} :answer-field/id #uuid "f29d4169-0dcc-5881-9d8b-1107b9cc1f1c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369449}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7834afec-6e2d-5917-b360-ab0e90141806" :question/math-academy-id "q-126858" :question/problem "![](images/52/52ec0dfc4590daf9306c2389bd321acbf80f263c799fa4ddd4e5b8f662e0a2b5.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C={C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2},$ where \n\n- ${C}_{1}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{1}(t)={t}^{5}\\,i+{t}^{4}\\,j$ for $t\\in [-1,1],$ and\n\n- ${C}_{2}$ is parametrized by ${r}_{2}(t)=-t\\,i+1\\,j$ for $t\\in [-1,1].$\n\nUse Green's theorem to find the area of the region." :question/worked-solution "First, let $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$\n\nOur simple, piecewise-smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivatives of ${r}_{1}(t)$ and ${r}_{2}(t)$ are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1}:\\, & {r}_{1}^{′}(t)=5{t}^{4}\\,i+4{t}^{3}\\,j \\\\ {C}_{2}:\\, & {r}_{2}^{′}(t)=-i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}\\cup {C}_{2}}x\\,dy \\\\  & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}x\\,dy+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}x\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{5}\\cdot 4{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(-t)\\cdot 0\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{8}\\,\\text{d}t-0 \\\\  & =4[\\frac{{t}^{9}}{9}]_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{2}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369455 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74b43056-e8dd-536d-b56a-12ac2800c12b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "364" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369457} {:answer/id #uuid "ca09836f-3c6e-5b5f-841d-68ede18363c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "215" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369458} {:answer/id #uuid "ac3caf47-5329-54b1-8c87-bcf0da3ef96b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "424" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369459} {:answer/id #uuid "b9b5b1dc-7bc7-5bb9-8b23-063bc496f471" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "342" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369460} {:answer/id #uuid "1d2e77d0-1779-5c5c-9e64-d3cabd705415" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "124" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369461}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "74b43056-e8dd-536d-b56a-12ac2800c12b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "364" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369457} :answer-field/id #uuid "175c17d1-364f-5ab7-a6b1-2ce4de237b97" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369456}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "017f8f34-6e37-562a-87cf-df5a06a7e434" :question/math-academy-id "q-126860" :question/problem "![](images/b5/b5a42039e2501a3909062fd6ed92e0879df03f58fa85a62b5e738e8abc78b903.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}8x\\,dy-5y\\,dx,\n$$ where $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-5y$ and $Q(x,y)=8x.$ So, \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-5,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}8x\\,dy-5y\\,dx & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(8-(-5))\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}13\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =13{∬}_{D}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =13\\cdot \\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =13\\cdot ({8}^{2}-{6}^{2}) \\\\  & =13\\cdot (64-36) \\\\  & =13\\cdot 28 \\\\  & =364.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369462 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d7cf9a0-e8a9-528c-9faf-b2259025de3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369464} {:answer/id #uuid "ce1af42a-4bf9-5730-a278-44c2bcb271c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369465} {:answer/id #uuid "26f52db4-19ad-52bc-bdea-a43ddccabe76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369466} {:answer/id #uuid "74b55cd4-e132-5c25-af68-783e5644f0be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369467} {:answer/id #uuid "5901a191-1792-51d7-92cf-8ae7fe658d89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369468}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5901a191-1792-51d7-92cf-8ae7fe658d89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369468} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0c368b0-4e71-50a8-b81a-cf6d68ab083b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369463}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ca63e980-b1ab-5e66-b1ce-ccb3144f7558" :question/math-academy-id "q-126862" :question/problem "![](images/49/49b14da54c952d0585f7aa894d618838ddc91beb65216e0c18c014ba5ad268d7.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=9,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-4,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-6,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ is oriented counterclockwise and ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented clockwise, calculate the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/a8/a8e93055fb9a460ab7757475c9c381c191204eae72231921da870b8d727bf988.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =9+(-4)+(-6) \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369469 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0b304ac-2b36-5437-b9d8-5fcf23fa4d1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369471} {:answer/id #uuid "a879fd91-6ea0-5ab3-ad43-e4706f2d1bc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369472} {:answer/id #uuid "b0f30d72-4cc9-59bd-b370-c87bbec40944" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369473} {:answer/id #uuid "a8834855-579a-54da-8ae2-aa6847585195" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369474} {:answer/id #uuid "253604d5-d42a-5d84-ab1b-eb721ad3403c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369475}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8834855-579a-54da-8ae2-aa6847585195" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369474} :answer-field/id #uuid "671458dc-075f-5c3e-9fa3-8839124a034e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369470}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9f6dbe2c-628b-581d-a31e-93679dd1a545" :question/math-academy-id "q-126863" :question/problem "![](images/b3/b3562daf036c74c1472931a626d41f28de81e723377597700342823d7f2d1ac2.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=1,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=7,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-4,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ is oriented counterclockwise and ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented clockwise, calculate the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/52/527eb8572b90c3d2a4449b69015a9b94f4d121b094be6629e1b92c45f2ff0eb0.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =1+7+(-4) \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369476 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e510a18e-355c-5760-a4ed-c27a7d87f4e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369478} {:answer/id #uuid "57708075-d434-55d2-9a8f-50cf869df4aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369479} {:answer/id #uuid "ef5552ef-88b6-5aae-9816-a98a72f5bfb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369480} {:answer/id #uuid "e2e548cf-aab0-5f75-aa5e-55e9f6b0ab38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369481} {:answer/id #uuid "a2ba05f7-beee-5f04-830c-1ff34eb38cd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369482}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2e548cf-aab0-5f75-aa5e-55e9f6b0ab38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369481} :answer-field/id #uuid "4bd217e4-f838-5e2c-9390-01eaef7a99cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369477}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "123e0e8e-27c7-50ce-bca4-e02b7470e632" :question/math-academy-id "q-126865" :question/problem "![](images/00/00edab4b102cf705ea6c65acaa61ba524e9dba5b503167d6988b2150802d9f0b.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=7,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-9,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented counterclockwise and ${C}_{2}$ is oriented clockwise, calculate the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/be/be04b9b505d71a99f7333ca9e9e9755c78453421f5aaf197306cbc5f2ca8df63.png)\n\nNotice that the positive orientation of the inner curve ${C}_{3}$ is opposite that of ${C}_{3}$ as stated in the question.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =7+(-9)-1 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369483 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a1d2c6b-2930-525d-8d87-b98d293ac862" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369485} {:answer/id #uuid "b507d870-7a81-57fc-907a-7e0a5f10c87d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369486} {:answer/id #uuid "26edd21f-0fb1-5cee-a037-f2ac92849637" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369487} {:answer/id #uuid "c5449ea3-86e0-567c-994b-5a2e62f052d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369488} {:answer/id #uuid "7fa50445-3382-50ec-b369-94e7f20cec60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369489}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26edd21f-0fb1-5cee-a037-f2ac92849637" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369487} :answer-field/id #uuid "66710c87-9546-58da-b5cb-2d949ea2fa53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369484}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b1e9162c-9f73-505c-8f10-aceab91f0c27" :question/math-academy-id "q-126867" :question/problem "![](images/b2/b2754f286de30f2017974030e571ec52f85bb84ad633912922a2b514728f3a7b.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=6,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=2,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-10,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ is oriented clockwise and ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented counterclockwise, calculate the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/85/85a8ea287d2cb8e3b70e332ac444a9e06262fef35369d1bad1f24ea5c9d31687.png)\n\nNotice that the positive orientations of the outer and inner curves are opposite those stated in the question.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =-6-2-(-10) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369490 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19b2f076-3c1b-5b41-b54d-acbf22c85f9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369492} {:answer/id #uuid "dcf840d4-a3b7-5a1f-8ab9-000eaa11d729" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369493} {:answer/id #uuid "544f331d-d37a-57d2-a8ec-3849a346d73c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369494} {:answer/id #uuid "7953c459-d75a-5d7a-a051-42cf152ae33e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369495} {:answer/id #uuid "fc7aedd6-9474-596f-83c6-b28c824b5114" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369496}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7953c459-d75a-5d7a-a051-42cf152ae33e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369495} :answer-field/id #uuid "998a54d8-ef67-567f-aff6-05f6543bcc9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369491}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d9bfcd0d-e2e4-5ddd-944e-eb308c18a16d" :question/math-academy-id "q-126870" :question/problem "![](images/1f/1f308d1a3ecf3bd0090e22c4f70389311db4db3ff06f7038db0cf7770a8837c7.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=5,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-2,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-9,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ and ${C}_{2}$ are oriented clockwise and ${C}_{3}$ is oriented counterclockwise, calculate the value of $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/2b/2bed1b367cd101e0e90674c619ad87da8c90744df570eeea4d9b0333ccee0de1.png)\n\nNotice that the positive orientations of the outer curve ${C}_{1}$ and the inner curve ${C}_{3}$ are opposite those stated in the question.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & =-{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r-{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =-5+(-2)-(-9) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369497 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e6750b8-75e8-5604-bdf3-5b26e689ff79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369499} {:answer/id #uuid "71c2554d-1b8f-50ee-904e-bf572ea954dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369500} {:answer/id #uuid "d3d1e293-45b2-553f-8738-81af539d167d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369501} {:answer/id #uuid "808c516e-8fb0-5d9d-b3c3-71bd5e0c0d18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369502} {:answer/id #uuid "56bd1130-5922-503b-9779-186620077dd1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369503}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3d1e293-45b2-553f-8738-81af539d167d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369501} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a4245ec-3053-53c4-bb04-e49df5e84b57" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369498}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c3c22304-91b2-51e6-94d5-71e3689e1379" :question/math-academy-id "q-126894" :question/problem "![](images/0e/0e86380c457bc9d37d0180057d4ce8c035055986d32254d95fab3a69254531a5.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}6y\\,dx+9x\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=6y$ and $Q(x,y)=9x.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=6,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=9.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}dx+x\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(9-6)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =3\\,\\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =3\\cdot (\\pi \\cdot {3}^{2}-\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 5\\pi  \\\\  & =15\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369504 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6a80b35-ba1d-5225-b811-45d42e3518c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{80\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369506} {:answer/id #uuid "e3d29cd2-757a-5f31-a966-786ef1201ef4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{242\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369507} {:answer/id #uuid "9a7bf771-68a8-5635-b443-270be3859a67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{728\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369508} {:answer/id #uuid "3d1b880d-8ca7-5039-a151-5e9c415014b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "728\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369509} {:answer/id #uuid "db63a2b7-a407-5f7d-b9b2-4abcfc16c974" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "243\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369510}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9a7bf771-68a8-5635-b443-270be3859a67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{728\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369508} :answer-field/id #uuid "0fe2c0c3-ccf0-5837-bf7d-eba354f732ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369505}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a38e1386-54d8-53c8-80be-ec42c2b70086" :question/math-academy-id "q-126935" :question/problem "![](images/5c/5ca9f071eaeebb0b31fba99a48ca8f08bd6b4b73588838aad7b5f513c283fd6c.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}-({x}^{4}y+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}{y}^{3})\\,dx+(x{y}^{4}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}{y}^{2})\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-{x}^{4}y-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}{y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=x{y}^{4}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{x}^{4}-{x}^{2}{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}={y}^{4}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}-({x}^{4}y+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}{y}^{3})\\,dx+(x{y}^{4}+\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{y}^{4}+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}-(-{x}^{4}-{x}^{2}{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{4}+2{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+{y}^{4}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})^{2}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{4}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{5}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{5}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{5}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot {\\frac{1}{6}{r}^{6}|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot ({3}^{6}-{1}^{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{728\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369511 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "adb00ccb-3eb2-54ed-b39e-a1b797a275e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-90\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369513} {:answer/id #uuid "7353f729-db5d-5849-9168-5e4b921191db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369514} {:answer/id #uuid "f2684cd6-1f99-5ede-94b6-e3ceba6e28d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-145\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369515} {:answer/id #uuid "f2d8e429-0275-503d-8cb9-59c096c6a40c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-120\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369516} {:answer/id #uuid "1b918db5-2ba3-5ac0-8eae-fc4b0f13cbc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369517}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2d8e429-0275-503d-8cb9-59c096c6a40c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-120\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369516} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d326558-bc4e-5ef3-84ac-5f28a471d7e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369512}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "88b73727-8c06-5f8a-b431-35e41d39a38b" :question/math-academy-id "q-126937" :question/problem "![](images/e6/e6252037001e1a5548ede86a16b4ddde8f5aec8fd956bded55a3506f7b533dbe.png)\n\nCalculate $$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}(\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,dx+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({sin}^{2}⁡y)-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})\\,dy,\n$$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({sin}^{2}⁡y)-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}={y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}(\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,dx+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({sin}^{2}⁡y)-\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,2\\le r\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi }.\n$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{2}^{4}-((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{2}^{4}-{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{2}^{4}-{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{2}^{4}-{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =-2\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =-2\\pi \\cdot {\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot ({4}^{4}-{2}^{4}) \\\\  & =-120\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369518 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d05767d-a65e-5d43-a488-57f4105f6090" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369520} {:answer/id #uuid "b9437141-8677-5d36-ade8-56553767eb3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369521} {:answer/id #uuid "71c53a26-6b18-5e5a-a6b4-14d20c2a1939" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369522} {:answer/id #uuid "c90c6307-93a1-5a42-b489-784db15ed739" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369523} {:answer/id #uuid "d963956c-f4e3-525a-b6b9-91fd86eb5922" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369524}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d05767d-a65e-5d43-a488-57f4105f6090" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369520} :answer-field/id #uuid "38c1ef24-72d4-585c-9c7b-034e01ca1112" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369519}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ae4fa615-3a59-509f-96cf-981693bf3db2" :question/math-academy-id "q-126941" :question/problem "![](images/b2/b232a4cddf05a425709c1b3204b69b0126e586b42a6af73b867dd75ee294d961.png)\n\nCalculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}({x}^{3}{y}^{3}+x)\\,dx+({x}^{4}{y}^{2}-y)\\,dy,\n$$\nwhere $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{3}{y}^{3}+x$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{4}{y}^{2}-y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=3{x}^{3}{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4{x}^{3}{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}({x}^{3}{y}^{3}+x)\\,dx+({x}^{4}{y}^{2}-y)\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4{x}^{3}{y}^{2}-3{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in Cartesian coordinates as ${Δ}_{1}∖{Δ}_{2},$ where\n\n- ${Δ}_{1}={(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,-1\\le y\\le 2}$ is the region enclosed by the outer curve, and\n\n- ${Δ}_{2}={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,0\\le y\\le 1}$ is the region enclosed by the inner curve.\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{Δ}_{1}}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{{Δ}_{2}}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}\\,{|}_{-2}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}\\,{|}_{-1}^{2}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}\\,{|}_{0}^{1}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{3}}{3}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(4-4)\\cdot (\\frac{8}{3}-\\left(-\\frac{1}{3}\\right))-(\\frac{1}{4}-0)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}-0) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 3-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{12}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186369525 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "006e746d-7483-59b3-ba9c-8cd280691f9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369527} {:answer/id #uuid "64fc0003-b805-567e-8d14-5ab416216a60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369528} {:answer/id #uuid "14561ae1-e3be-5f9d-bccd-fde3e73d6059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369529} {:answer/id #uuid "da7adf00-3485-5b42-ac23-7efeb71d22ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369530} {:answer/id #uuid "89cc8f37-3c67-5183-a3bc-6cf27168df13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369531}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89cc8f37-3c67-5183-a3bc-6cf27168df13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369531} :answer-field/id #uuid "1303c02b-a3ee-52e9-98a2-763dbec18e4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186369526}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "904872cd-e02c-55d9-9792-17de482b0907" :question/math-academy-id "q-126945" :question/problem "![](images/75/754aa1ded10b20362350f3da9e5b4dbe5f9f194c2289af348ff9de34f2fb8cc5.png)\n\nCalculate \n$$\n\\displaystyle {∮}_{∂D}(x{y}^{2}+{x}^{3})\\,dx+(2{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3})\\,dy,\n$$\nwhere $D$ is the region shown above." :question/worked-solution "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=x{y}^{2}+{x}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)=2{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=4xy.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}(x{y}^{2}+{x}^{3})\\,dx+(2{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4xy-2xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in Cartesian coordinates as ${Δ}_{1}∖{Δ}_{2},$ where\n\n- ${Δ}_{1}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,-1\\le y\\le 2\\}$ is the region enclosed by the outer curve, and\n\n- ${Δ}_{2}=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,0\\le y\\le 1\\}$ is the region enclosed by the inner curve.\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}2xy\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{{Δ}_{1}}2xy\\,\\text{d}A-{∬}_{{Δ}_{2}}2xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}2xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}2xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}2x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{-1}^{2}y\\,\\text{d}y-{\\int }_{-1}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{1}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\,{|}_{-2}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{-1}^{2}-{x}^{2}\\,{|}_{-1}^{1}\\cdot \\frac{{y}^{2}}{2}\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(4-4)\\cdot (2-\\frac{1}{2})-(1-1)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}-0) \\\\  & =0\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}-0\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186370913 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2af7955-e8f7-5d06-9494-41b60d32dda6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370915} {:answer/id #uuid "eeb61e07-30d9-5656-b689-8f8a3ed177fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6({sin}^{2}⁡t-{cos}^{2}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370916} {:answer/id #uuid "08be8790-c0ff-5547-af1e-b5707ffe8884" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6({cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370917} {:answer/id #uuid "050b2982-51ab-5396-940b-7dbb8bae2852" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{sin}^{2}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370918} {:answer/id #uuid "d2c4546b-9878-58e1-b16e-060b263f44e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{cos}^{2}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d2c4546b-9878-58e1-b16e-060b263f44e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{cos}^{2}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370919} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0c958a9-3def-549d-9734-898a0cb36e97" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186370914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "784069b0-0cb9-51e0-bbfa-d48bc8a07132" :question/math-academy-id "q-41466" :question/problem "![](images/1d/1de9544b8da50f6280920adf2292da517a844b8b4143f58f646da0a01ffad16c.png)\n\nLet $D$ be the region enclosed by the curve $C$ parametrized as\n$r(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$ for $t\\in [0,2\\pi ].$ The area of $D$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})dA={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\boxed{}\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhere $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x.$ Determine the missing part of the equation." :question/worked-solution "Our simple, smooth, closed path $C$ is positively oriented, and  \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1-0=1.\n$$\n\nSo, according to Green's theorem, we can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSubstituting $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x,$ we get\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y={∮}_{C}x\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\n \n\nThe derivative of $r(t)$ is\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)=⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our line integral as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}x\\,dy & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,dt \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }6{cos}^{2}⁡t\\,dt.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371721 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0c3c144-342c-568c-8008-dfc3bbf3e55b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371723} {:answer/id #uuid "819bb05a-265c-5492-a6f2-63e69e3366e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371724} {:answer/id #uuid "7cc99bc0-2cfc-57b8-ad76-ee0deba98fe7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371725} {:answer/id #uuid "9773cc34-e79f-5a4a-b9ef-d818a89d1a1e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371726} {:answer/id #uuid "e1a2a18e-b24b-5257-8c86-2b702af0f038" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371727}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0c3c144-342c-568c-8008-dfc3bbf3e55b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371723} :answer-field/id #uuid "af468363-83b9-5ccc-8120-0215969486e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186371722}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0760b1bf-45a5-5aed-9a7d-860971967403" :question/math-academy-id "q-127633" :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve $r(t)=⟨t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(1)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}t\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371735 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2067362d-b2dd-5b81-a0c0-254679d504c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470973} {:answer/id #uuid "85d673ae-d427-529a-a86e-84c5f3aca678" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470974}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2067362d-b2dd-5b81-a0c0-254679d504c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470973} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d2487c1-305d-5d73-b58d-603c12dd9b35" :answer-field/key "$c=-1$ gives the $x$-axis, excluding the origin" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471992} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cf13b53-53c5-595b-b162-9c02134b389f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470975} {:answer/id #uuid "3d4fee56-e3c7-5fe0-b2bf-af7d6065e33d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2cf13b53-53c5-595b-b162-9c02134b389f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470975} :answer-field/id #uuid "6697dd78-1361-5fb4-a196-6b0e79a6e315" :answer-field/key "$c=0$ gives the $y$-axis, excluding the origin" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471993} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b9766d54-18d4-55a2-a11d-25dda37f5384" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470977} {:answer/id #uuid "e67bade9-f7b9-51f8-8efc-a33eb81a712c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470978}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9766d54-18d4-55a2-a11d-25dda37f5384" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470977} :answer-field/id #uuid "7014990a-205c-5f78-a1f4-b40b21737c3d" :answer-field/key "$c=1$ gives the line $y=x,$ excluding the origin" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471994} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab14c2a6-a598-5ac5-839a-eebd57d6f315" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669021} {:answer/id #uuid "189f0029-79a2-5c16-a9a2-6a0b0264c926" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "189f0029-79a2-5c16-a9a2-6a0b0264c926" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669022} :answer-field/id #uuid "69eb2f7b-dfda-55c8-813d-ba545e514a58" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669310}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ac11e3e9-cb1c-5541-98a0-caa6e8b5b278" :question/math-academy-id "q-129059" :question/problem "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x+y}{x}$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{x} & =c \\\\ x+y & =cx \\\\ y & =(c-1)x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=-1,$ the level curve is the line $y=-2x,$ excluding the origin. So, “$c=-1$ gives the $x$-axis, excluding the origin” is false.\n\n- If $c=0,$ the level curve is the line $y=-x,$ excluding the origin. So, “$c=0$ gives the $y$-axis, excluding the origin” is false.\n\n- If $c=1,$ the level curve is $y=0,$ which is the $x$-axis (excluding the origin). So, “$c=1$ gives the line $y=x,$ excluding the origin” is false.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None of the listed\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186371742 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d59b3d7b-2c37-5359-93f8-d192269d5067" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470979} {:answer/id #uuid "ae0bb8f9-8485-5cfe-ab25-043cbc708f51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470980}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae0bb8f9-8485-5cfe-ab25-043cbc708f51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470980} :answer-field/id #uuid "146a2344-578d-5672-a271-50b88fd4f3f6" :answer-field/key "$c=0$ gives the point $(0,0)$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471995} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93422712-e5a0-55d3-b546-eb39f8114c5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470981} {:answer/id #uuid "381719ea-dfe9-56ca-b1c4-648a69f283b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470982}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "381719ea-dfe9-56ca-b1c4-648a69f283b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470982} :answer-field/id #uuid "965cc361-b43b-525a-b426-5e5b2f07eb8e" :answer-field/key "$c=1$ gives a circle of radius $1$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471996} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26a45c3b-1449-5b78-a19d-8f000aabeace" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470983} {:answer/id #uuid "1e276396-1a9d-5de8-bbc0-32373a1fa78a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470984}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26a45c3b-1449-5b78-a19d-8f000aabeace" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186470983} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6cfc70f-9735-5a2a-bc4c-91a5d4a564a7" :answer-field/key "$c=4$ gives a parabola" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471997} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a7eb091-edbd-50ba-a6cb-ec27463cdeb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669023} {:answer/id #uuid "e3414ae3-ccd2-5598-8fd7-2e7fee239aba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669024}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a7eb091-edbd-50ba-a6cb-ec27463cdeb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669023} :answer-field/id #uuid "eac97bf4-38a1-5a49-9bc5-6a9528c16031" :answer-field/key "None of the above" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186669311}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ee3c74bd-cd07-5dd0-b26f-4b93aa9647b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-129061" :question/problem "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & =c \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={c}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=0,$ the level curve is $x=y=0,$ which gives the point $(0,0).$ So, “$c=0$ gives the point $(0,0)$” is true.\n\n- If $c=1,$ the level curve is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1,$ which gives a circle of radius $1.$ So, “$c=1$ gives a circle of radius $1$” is true.\n\n- If $c=4,$ the level curve is ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=16,$ which gives a circle of radius $4.$ So, “$c=4$ gives a parabola” is false.\n\nSo, the correct answer is “$c=0$ gives the point $(0,0)$”; “$c=1$ gives a circle of radius $1$” only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373132 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de1b2908-d140-5d0a-8262-c958d76bfa4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471471} {:answer/id #uuid "f456143a-b430-5e18-95eb-88219103f545" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471472}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de1b2908-d140-5d0a-8262-c958d76bfa4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471471} :answer-field/id #uuid "06e697f7-446e-5fcc-ae8a-db378503de70" :answer-field/key "$c=0$ gives an ellipse" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472241} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55e41eb5-2115-5b1f-9bbb-e67f510d8e1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471473} {:answer/id #uuid "bf770033-4a57-5465-a9ee-d6d68d54b733" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471474}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf770033-4a57-5465-a9ee-d6d68d54b733" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471474} :answer-field/id #uuid "83576e31-5a8a-5315-8054-127553d4c6a1" :answer-field/key "$c=-4$ gives a line" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472242} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ad5d90de-a61d-5e9f-a889-d499427c60be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471475} {:answer/id #uuid "9f5c3a1e-f107-5159-ae8f-6ce7a0d2da41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471476}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad5d90de-a61d-5e9f-a889-d499427c60be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186471475} :answer-field/id #uuid "5771a531-83c1-5b90-a459-0ec86b28bfd9" :answer-field/key "$c=5$ gives a hyperbola" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186472243}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c853fbcf-5be0-5101-8f2a-558cbd4a782f" :question/math-academy-id "q-71402" :question/problem "The level curves of the function $f(x,y)=9x+3y-4$ are given by $f(x,y)=c.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Setting $f(x,y)=c$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9x+3y-4 & =c \\\\ 3y & =-9x+c+4 \\\\ y & =-3x+\\frac{c+4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n- If $c=0$ then the level curve is $y=-3x+\\frac{4}{3},$ which is not an ellipse.\n\n- If $c=-4$ then the level curve is $y=-3x,$ which is a line.\n\n- If $c=5$ then the level curve is $y=-3x+3,$ which is not a hyperbola.\n\nSo, only statement “$c=-4$ gives a line” is true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373223 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a712676f-2c97-5cb7-966d-09797a5f8021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373225} {:answer/id #uuid "cc3eecdc-de51-5ae0-8de8-01a1ae198ae8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373226} {:answer/id #uuid "21bd52ca-4869-53d6-8d7a-03019261b5ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373227} {:answer/id #uuid "70e3eb1f-dcfb-53cc-99f6-b1609cba2012" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373228} {:answer/id #uuid "f5d56a9c-d543-5f82-9abd-1d18c7ec7d92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373229}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5d56a9c-d543-5f82-9abd-1d18c7ec7d92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373229} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f0cc1ce-5d3d-51df-bd70-280476103aae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373224}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c6a58127-1393-5bb0-acf9-ab77e9d7ffad" :question/math-academy-id "q-76909" :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve $r(t)=⟨2-{t}^{2},\\,{t}^{2}+4,\\,\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}⟩$\nfrom  $t=0$ to $t=4.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+4),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2t,\\,2t,\\,t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-2t)}^{2}+{(2t)}^{2}+{(t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =3t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $t\\ge 0$ for $t\\in [0,4].$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}3t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}({t}^{2}){|}_{0}^{4} \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373230 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ffcfd43-b15b-55e0-9453-963134a6d995" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "130" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373232} {:answer/id #uuid "2e9391e4-7424-54d0-a36c-f5b21aa3b4e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "150" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373233} {:answer/id #uuid "726ddffe-91b8-5f33-bfb8-b9930883abab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "125" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373234} {:answer/id #uuid "4048f826-7e00-5c3f-ad4e-3b51a78539bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373235} {:answer/id #uuid "4ab3aac1-31a4-5103-a8fc-ee5e47c2090b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "90" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373236}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ffcfd43-b15b-55e0-9453-963134a6d995" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "130" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373232} :answer-field/id #uuid "f96bcd49-a02f-5bd7-ab21-7a05bacf2ad7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373231}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5e0a56e4-79a9-58b5-babc-ce3937cee4f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-76910" :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve $r(t)=⟨\\frac{4-\\sqrt{3}{t}^{2}}{\\sqrt{2}},\\,{t}^{3},\\,t+5⟩$\nfrom  $t=0$ to $t=5.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{4-\\sqrt{3}{t}^{2}}{\\sqrt{2}}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+5)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\sqrt{6}t,\\,3{t}^{2},\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-\\sqrt{6}t)}^{2}+{(3{t}^{2})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6{t}^{2}+9{t}^{4}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(1+3{t}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =1+3{t}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{5}(1+3{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t+{t}^{3}){|}_{0}^{5} \\\\  & =5+125-0 \\\\  & =130.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373965 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5477038f-1f6e-56c9-abc2-d2382dd8493b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7uv-4v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373967} {:answer/id #uuid "938e31d9-0fe9-5c52-a191-c068034a6287" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11u-24v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373968} {:answer/id #uuid "73e0a3dc-0594-5177-ad29-6281fad0f7ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11u+4v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373969} {:answer/id #uuid "9cd2d79e-1a1c-53cf-b530-bcceff073bb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u-v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373970} {:answer/id #uuid "1188f498-8136-53fa-a7c5-2ca872f32cf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24uv" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373971}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "938e31d9-0fe9-5c52-a191-c068034a6287" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11u-24v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373968} :answer-field/id #uuid "f789f76e-872e-5abd-b18f-ca246f894f3b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "acc5680d-481f-574e-9c37-7723114fd2f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-161381" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z+x,\\,y+2z,\\,x-4y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=2x-4y$ for $-2\\le x\\le 0$ and $-1\\le y\\le 5.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $F$ through $S$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{-1}^{5}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,2u-4v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 0,\\,-1\\le v\\le 5\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(2u-4v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,2⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(2u-4v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -4\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & -4\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,\\,4,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨z+x,\\,y+2z,\\,x-4y⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=2u-4v} \\\\  & =⟨2u-4v+u,\\,v+2(2u-4v),\\,u-4v⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3u-4v,\\,4u-7v,\\,u-4v⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨3u-4v,\\,4u-7v,\\,u-4v⟩\\cdot ⟨-2,\\,4,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(3u-4v)\\cdot (-2)+(4u-7v)\\cdot 4+(u-4v)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-6u+8v+16u-28v+u-4v \\\\  & =11u-24v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨z+x,\\,y+2z,\\,x-4y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(11u-24v)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{0}{\\int }_{-1}^{5}(11u-24v)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373972 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35f2e086-31b9-544a-9b9a-99a08a27f04e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373974} {:answer/id #uuid "291af077-cf81-5322-8799-9c4d0a56816f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373975} {:answer/id #uuid "84fcb1f0-4936-5fbf-afb7-a71a059f4960" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373976} {:answer/id #uuid "c8544813-339e-5ed8-a745-c0b3a602e975" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373977} {:answer/id #uuid "c730413b-a101-500a-a942-8a345613f8ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373978}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "291af077-cf81-5322-8799-9c4d0a56816f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373975} :answer-field/id #uuid "05d45f19-fa05-53a9-92e1-77a6078e07fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373973}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9a86f1df-9194-5ee6-ac19-2bfbeca02663" :question/math-academy-id "q-161447" :question/problem "Evaluate the surface integral\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}⟨y,\\,x+z,\\,z+2x⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S\n$$\n\nwhere the surface $S$ is defined by $z=y-2x$ for $1\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe first compute the partial derivatives of the graph $z=g(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂g}{∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-2x)=-2 \\\\ \\frac{∂g}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y-2x)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the equation $z=g(x,y)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x,y,g(x,y)) & =⟨y,\\,x+z,\\,z+2x⟩{|}_{z=y-2x} \\\\  & =⟨y,\\,x+(y-2x),\\,y-2x+2x⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\underbrace{y}_{P},\\,\\underbrace{y-x}_{Q},\\,\\underbrace{y}_{R}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can evaluate our surface integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨y,\\,x+z,\\,z+2x⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-y\\cdot (-2)-(y-x)\\cdot (1)+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2y-y+x+y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2y+x)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2y+x)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{[{y}^{2}+xy]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}(1+x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[x+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =2+2-1-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373979 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "333cd0c0-ea57-5e53-975d-79f76fb18013" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373981} {:answer/id #uuid "9ac4e1a7-5360-5598-85dd-70d2cbb3cd86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-P,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373982} {:answer/id #uuid "1e3427ae-61e2-5bd9-9b34-c9a3c4f9f4ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-P,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂y},\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373983} {:answer/id #uuid "d4c19d76-6f69-56ba-b90f-4e4dfe1d7922" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-P,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373984} {:answer/id #uuid "24bbe4da-5bb4-5f04-bd1a-ef46ed95ade9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-P,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂y},\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373985}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4c19d76-6f69-56ba-b90f-4e4dfe1d7922" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-P,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373984} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c9eea5b-e0c0-5c9a-bb50-8a16e942de63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373980}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "54d448ec-919c-5c61-a65c-48a634770024" :question/math-academy-id "q-161460" :question/problem "For a surface $S$ given by $y=h(x,z)$ for $(x,z)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\,\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}+Q-R\\,\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?\n\n*Hint: The orientation of $S$ should be chosen such that the unit normal vector has a positive  $j$-component.*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $y=h(x,z),$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(x,z)=⟨x,\\,h(x,z),\\,z⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨1,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂x},\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨0,\\frac{∂h}{∂z},\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & \\frac{∂h}{∂x} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{∂h}{∂z} & 1\\end{vmatrix}=⟨\\frac{∂h}{∂x},\\,-1,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂z}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, notice that the $j$-component is negative. Therefore, we select the normal vector that points in the *opposite* direction:\n\n$$\n{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{x}^{′}=-{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′}=⟨-\\frac{∂h}{∂x},\\,1,\\,-\\frac{∂h}{∂z}⟩\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(x,z))\\cdot ({r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{∂h}{∂x},\\,1,\\,-\\frac{∂h}{∂z}⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂x}+Q-R\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂z})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373986 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f202e15-0fbe-50af-9841-523a939ec510" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373988} {:answer/id #uuid "176843b4-510b-5e14-95d1-94f7f1d2b76b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u-2v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373989} {:answer/id #uuid "5fb6d1ec-f761-53b6-be0f-80e6cadaee49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8u-2v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373990} {:answer/id #uuid "73b04bf4-208b-5a5c-aac6-b4ba092785fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373991} {:answer/id #uuid "eeb5f55b-ba54-5712-9575-db91590f82a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2u+4v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373992}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fb6d1ec-f761-53b6-be0f-80e6cadaee49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8u-2v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373990} :answer-field/id #uuid "1a967ea9-62a2-59e7-9156-ea0fff576b7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373987}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "94077de1-f15b-584e-aa54-5f345805312b" :question/math-academy-id "q-161467" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨2x-z,\\,y-z,\\,z+y⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by $z=4x-y$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $-1\\le y\\le 2.$ Using the parametrization $x=u$ and $y=v,$ the flux of $F$ through $S$ can be expressed as \n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\boxed{}\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nWhat is the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nUsing the parametrization, where $x=u$ and $y=v,$ our surface can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(u,v)=⟨u,\\,v,\\,4u-v⟩\n$$\n\nover the domain \n\n$$\nD=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,-1\\le v\\le 2\\}.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂u},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂u}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂u}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂u}(4u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,4⟩ \\\\ {r}_{v}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂x}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂y}{∂v},\\,\\frac{∂z}{∂v}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂v}(u),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(v),\\,\\frac{∂}{∂v}(4u-v)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′} & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =⟨\\begin{vmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\,-\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{vmatrix},\\,\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{vmatrix}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4,\\,1,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the coordinate expressions of $r(u,v)$ into $F(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v)) & =⟨2x-z,\\,y-z,\\,z+y⟩{|}_{x=u,\\,\\,y=v,\\,\\,z=4u-v} \\\\  & =⟨2u-(4u-v),\\,v-(4u-v),\\,4u-v+v⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2u+v,\\,-4u+2v,\\,4u⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the dot product of $F$ and ${r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′}) & =⟨-2u+v,\\,-4u+2v,\\,4u⟩\\cdot ⟨-4,\\,1,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =(-2u+v)\\cdot (-4)+(-4u+2v)\\cdot 1+4u\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =8u-4v+-4u+2v+4u \\\\  & =8u-2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}⟨2x-z,\\,y-z,\\,z+y⟩\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(8u-2v)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}(8u-2v)\\,\\text{d}v\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374186 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9dedb4d6-dae2-543b-80b2-9ccef9fa5a88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374188} {:answer/id #uuid "8eabaa4a-a54f-5ddc-885b-ce8690b34285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374189} {:answer/id #uuid "896566d9-4a61-5d61-b4f4-4aa45c0939f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374190} {:answer/id #uuid "4dc850a0-843d-56eb-abc2-2419b6e13be9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374191} {:answer/id #uuid "af363662-dfd8-5c09-8594-73404f30ea3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374192}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8eabaa4a-a54f-5ddc-885b-ce8690b34285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374189} :answer-field/id #uuid "9accbfc9-5746-540c-8cdb-b27e56facfc9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374187}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "139943e3-1c42-5e1e-8135-8c3ce7b2dffc" :question/math-academy-id "q-162570" :question/problem "![](images/98/9861e9edc3c7517af6fd912046f62ec04a6e478942c3017ed9903e8b366130c2.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨1-2x,\\,5y-3{z}^{2},\\,{x}^{3}-{y}^{2}⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $2$ whose base is a circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Given that $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1-2x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(5y-3{z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{3}-{y}^{2}) \\\\  & =-2+5+0 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid cylinder enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}3\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =3{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {3}^{2}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =54\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374193 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a718cce9-8e5d-5dec-8a0a-3840ae5b697b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374195} {:answer/id #uuid "b2dec189-d251-5cdb-beb7-7e7b56595d20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374196} {:answer/id #uuid "1af109f7-1025-5d70-baae-5d18a7702281" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374197} {:answer/id #uuid "a2591ccc-8c08-599f-b2a7-b656cee9d9f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374198} {:answer/id #uuid "0653f4e3-f779-564a-8100-ee0e0831159c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374199}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2dec189-d251-5cdb-beb7-7e7b56595d20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374196} :answer-field/id #uuid "24fbd410-95a8-5da8-9827-3827f59a3a95" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374194}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a61b4d5a-a480-5473-9f9f-c9865eee4653" :question/math-academy-id "q-162571" :question/problem "![](images/07/07b46ab683e54813e0f6090c6841302ea7d914bc6b1577afc883f6e42e0a3903.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F=⟨3x-y{z}^{2},\\,y+{e}^{z},\\,2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Given that $S$ is positively (outward) oriented, use the divergence theorem to find the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(3x-y{z}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y+{e}^{z})+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =3+1+0 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the rectangular solid enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}4\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =4{∭}_{R}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\text{Volume}(R) \\\\  & =4\\cdot 1\\cdot 2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374200 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25469fa9-aa9b-5ce2-912f-092bcf0c9271" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374202} {:answer/id #uuid "7ee396c4-83e0-5fa2-ae23-69048563ecb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374203} {:answer/id #uuid "ddac6d93-fada-5b42-9d98-dffa0367488c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374204} {:answer/id #uuid "c8406793-5096-5aff-b557-a3bebc57cf38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374205} {:answer/id #uuid "88de07a7-1a0f-5f7b-b1a1-718b8c6821c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374206}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ddac6d93-fada-5b42-9d98-dffa0367488c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374204} :answer-field/id #uuid "940992af-dba8-51a2-a024-f36c67d58c17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374201}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c1604058-cdd6-524d-a2be-ab81c147d2ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-162597" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=1,$ $x=3,$ $y=0,$ $y=2,$ $z=-1,$ $z=1.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=(y-{x}^{2})\\,i+xy\\,j+3xz\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y-{x}^{2})+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(3xz) \\\\  & =(-2x)+x+3x \\\\  & =2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}2x\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}2x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{2}]_{x=1}^{x=3}\\cdot (2-0)\\cdot (1-(-1)) \\\\  & =(9-1)\\cdot 2\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =32.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374207 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a1a9148-8e77-5639-ad7f-cbca7f0df0cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374209} {:answer/id #uuid "0bcc0510-4039-55e0-823c-e4ac7d5653d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374210} {:answer/id #uuid "0acfab67-9718-5e64-b967-a1914c71eddf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374211} {:answer/id #uuid "2bf62725-3d23-5464-bf59-4f18a980c4db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374212} {:answer/id #uuid "702faaa4-c282-5398-860f-c6e1f3c9bb1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a1a9148-8e77-5639-ad7f-cbca7f0df0cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374209} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ced5422-0477-54ea-a063-de1490b4e5d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374208}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c2a8924c-323c-54ea-b830-8522b9835da1" :question/math-academy-id "q-162602" :question/problem "Let $S$ be the boundary of the rectangular prism enclosed by the planes $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ $y=0,$ $y=3,$ $z=1,$ $z=3.$ Given that the surface $S$ is positively (outward) oriented and $F=(2{x}^{3}+z)\\,i-z\\,j-3{x}^{2}z\\,k,$ find the flux of $F$ across $S.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $R$ be a simple solid region bounded by a closed surface $S$ with positive (outward) orientation. If a vector field $F$ has continuous partial derivatives for all its components in an open region in space that contains $R,$ then the divergence theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS={∭}_{R}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nComputing the divergence of $F,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{div}\\,F & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{3}+z)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-z)+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(-3{x}^{2}z) \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}+0+(-3{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $R$ be the solid region enclosed by $S.$ Then, using the divergence theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot dS & ={∭}_{R}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}V \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{1}^{3}3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}3{x}^{2}\\text{d}x\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}\\text{d}y\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{3}\\text{d}z \\\\  & =[{x}^{3}]_{x=-1}^{x=1}\\cdot (3-0)\\cdot (3-1) \\\\  & =({1}^{3}-(-1)^{3})\\cdot 3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375624 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b587af18-df81-5db4-88ae-e75eb49804be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}={\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375626} {:answer/id #uuid "42dbc938-1217-5910-be74-c7acaaa09ee3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}={\\left(\\frac{2}{x}\\right)}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375627} {:answer/id #uuid "d880a9fe-7ede-58b5-95dc-503280226675" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}={\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375628} {:answer/id #uuid "c09ce9e9-e44a-5f6f-a0ba-e4f831714695" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}={x}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375629} {:answer/id #uuid "394ebbf2-c2d3-501a-9d24-4f85814641c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=1-{\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375630}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d880a9fe-7ede-58b5-95dc-503280226675" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}={\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375628} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5fabfe3-808f-535a-8196-05aa4185848e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375625}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8b4d7dbc-be4c-5d12-8115-af652723cf38" :question/math-academy-id "q-102677" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in (0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's make the trigonometric functions the subject of each equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2} \\\\ y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\, & ⇒\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡t=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDividing the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t=1$ by ${cos}^{2}⁡t$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡t}{{cos}^{2}⁡t}+\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t}{{cos}^{2}⁡t} & =\\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\ {\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\right)}^{2}+1 & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ {tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\operatorname{tan}⁡t=\\frac{x}{2}$ and $\\operatorname{sec}⁡t=y$ into this formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{2}+1={y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the Cartesian equation of the given curve is ${y}^{2}={\\left(\\frac{x}{2}\\right)}^{2}+1.$"}]}
